Found on Windows 2026-11-22 (Windows 11 26H2 build 26300.9711, Windows PowerShell 5.1, unreagent d4edd23) while probing k204.

Piping a console command into the launcher from Windows PowerShell 5.1  'quit' | unreagent.exe -config ...  reached the command loop with a byte order mark in front: the launcher answered "Unknown command: ?quit" (printed as "?quit") and kept running. On this machine $OutputEncoding of that PowerShell session is UTF-8 with BOM, so every stdin stream PowerShell writes to a native program starts with EF BB BF. Typed input in a console is not affected.

Fix candidate: strip a leading U+FEFF from the first line the command loop reads (cmd/launcher/lifecycle.go, the readLine in the console loop). Owner: unreagent-worker, with a test that feeds "\xef\xbb\xbfq\n". Low priority: it only bites scripted control from PowerShell.

2026-11-22 06:52 Measured on this machine (Windows PowerShell 5.1, piping into od.exe): with -NoProfile the session's $OutputEncoding is us-ascii and nothing carries a mark; with $OutputEncoding = [Text.Encoding]::UTF8, PowerShell writes EF BB BF exactly once, at byte 0 of the pipe. That holds for 'quit','status' and also for three objects sent 700 ms apart (& { 'quit'; Start-Sleep; 'status'; Start-Sleep; 'q' } | od): one mark, then CRLF lines.

Decision (mine): only the first line the console reader returns can carry the mark, so only that line loses it. A U+FEFF anywhere later is passed through as input. The fix lives in lineReader.readLine (cmd/launcher/lifecycle.go), not in commandLoop. lineReader is the session's one reader of the console input, so the exit prompt (promptAgentExit) gets the fix too: a piped 'k' or 'r' that is the first thing on stdin is no longer misread. If the foreground agent reads stdin first, it takes byte 0 and the mark with it, and the reader's first line has none to drop, so the strip does nothing.

Mechanics: a 'started' flag on lineReader. The first readLine drops the three bytes when they are exactly EF BB BF at the start of its line. It drops one mark only, and EOF semantics stay intact: a stream that is nothing but the mark reads as EOF ("", false), not as an empty line.

